description:
Also add braces to multi-function declaration if missing for `strict = TRUE` (closes #535):
``` r
library(styler)
style_text("function() h(~ { ... })")
#> function() {
#>   h(~ {
#>     ...
#>   })
#> }
```


<sup>Created on 2019-08-05 by the [reprex package](https://reprex.tidyverse.org) (v0.3.0)</sup>

Calls of that form had too much indention, see #535.
For `strict = FALSE`, no braces are added and the following cases were fixed to remain as is:

```r
if (x) h(
  x
) 

for (i in x) h(
  x
) 

while (x) h(
  x
) 

if (x) h(
  x
) else k(
  b
)
```

They all used to have the second line twice indented.

The solution is in almost entirely in 7c26637. Simply check if a new line follows after the closing brace. Does not need to use `needs_indention()` because if a token follows on `)` on the same line, it will do the indention if necessary, which is exactly the case if it is multi-line. The other cornerstone is to lower-bound indention at 0. Because `indent_without_paren_for_while_fun()` does not know if `strict = TRUE` and curly braces will be added later, a problem is created for function declarations where the body is not wrapped in `{...}`: (1) `indent_without_paren_for_while_fun()` does not indent on `FUNCTION` because `{` follows. (2) `wrap_subexpr_in_curly()` creates a curly expression from the body and wants to unindent the body, which was not indented. This creates negative indention. (3) serialization fails due to negative indention. This is avoided by lower-bounding the indention adjustment in `wrap_subexpr_in_curly()` to 0. It seemed more robust than trying to establish at runtime in `indent_without_paren_for_while_fun()` if `wrap_subexpr_in_curly()` will be called later.

# Overview
Token-dependent indention plays a minor role in styler, since the tidyvese style guide does only permit it in one situation: Function declarations.
As of now, token-dependent indention was always absolute, i.e. no other indention rules were allowed. This lead to problems such as the one outlined in #351. After having tried to solve the problem by modifying styler infrastructure (which was a hack, and committed in this branch for reference), the ideas developed in the second part of this PR (that reversed some hacks from the first part) should provide a more refined and flexible solution via a transformer function and allowing other style guides in the future to use the feature of token dependent indention in other ways.

# Example
Let's do it with an example. Suppose we have
```r
function(a = 
b, d +
e, 
c) {
  call(1, 
    2
)
}
```
Which is incorrectly indented.

Apply indention rules for braces (both curly and round). Function declarations will get indented because of the opening brace after `function` (no other indention is possible because other braces won't live in the same nest.
```r
function(a = 
  b, d,
  e, 
  c) {
  call(1, 
    2
)
}
```

Remove all indention in function declaration headers. Note that function calls are not affected. After this step, code may look like this:
```r
function(a = 
b, d,
e, 
c) {
  call(1, 
    2
)
}
```

Apply other indention rules such as indention based on operators (`+`, `=` etc.). Again, note that function calls are not affected.
```r
function(a = 
  b, d,
  e,
c) {
  call(1, 
    2
)
}
```
Finally, we add the indention of the reference token `(` to all tokens in the header, so we end up with the desired indention.
```r
function(a = 
           b, d,
         e,
        c) {
  call(1, 
    2
)
}
```

Closes #353, #352.

Similar to #21, where indention based on round brackets was added, this adds indention for curly brackets. However, there is one case that is not yet styled correctly (and the corresponding test fails on purpose). Spacing before curly brackets. Ideally, it would look like that:
```r
a <- function(x) {
  1
}
# or
if (x > 10) {
}
# and so on
```
The rule `add_space_before_brace()` remains ineffective since spacing is based on the concept of "space after tokens", i.e. spacing is added on the parse table to the column `spaces` the row before the token `{`. In the nested structure, there is no token before `{`, so no space is added. One way around that would be to add an empty token to every level of nesting so we can add spacing that way. This is pragmatic since we could still use all rules in R/rules.R, which are based on the assumption that the column `spaces` indicates the number of spaces after the token.
